WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.046 --> 00:00:08.770
The life of Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley began in tragedy.

2
00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:17.536
Born in London, England, on August 30, 1797, Mary lost her mother, the famous feminist Mary Wollstonecraft, to

3
00:00:17.556 --> 00:00:19.557
an infection 11 days after her birth.

4
00:00:20.578 --> 00:00:27.063
Although her father, the noted philosopher and novelist William Godwin, doted on Mary, she sorely missed the presence

5
00:00:27.123 --> 00:00:28.624
of her mother as she was growing up.

6
00:00:29.684 --> 00:00:35.248
Despite such a lonely beginning in life, Mary, who, as one biographer wrote, "entered the world like the

7
00:00:35.288 --> 00:00:41.333
heroine of a Gothic tale," became a remarkable individual whose writing has had a lasting impact on the

8
00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:47.537
world. Although she was not formally educated, Mary learned to read and write at a very young age.

9
00:00:48.738 --> 00:00:54.883
With access to her father's great library, she quickly became an independent scholar and writer, which was highly

10
00:00:54.943 --> 00:00:56.464
unusual for girls at the time.

11
00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:02.424
She enjoyed reading about a broad range of subjects and even had one of her own short stories

12
00:01:02.504 --> 00:01:05.166
published by her father's company when she was only eleven.

13
00:01:06.347 --> 00:01:08.949
Nevertheless, Mary's home life was not happy.

14
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:15.414
Her father remarried, and her new stepmother preferred her own children to Mary and was not kind or

15
00:01:15.494 --> 00:01:16.075
loving to her.

16
00:01:17.235 --> 00:01:21.279
At the age of fifteen, Mary was sent to live at the home of William Baxter, a family

17
00:01:21.339 --> 00:01:22.800
friend, in Dundee, Scotland.

18
00:01:23.764 --> 00:01:28.448
The Baxter home had a profound effect on Mary because it was her first experience living in a

19
00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:30.109
family with a loving mother and father.

20
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.434
On a visit home to London, Mary met a young poet named Percy Bysshe Shelley.

21
00:01:36.374 --> 00:01:41.158
Although Shelley was married and had a young daughter of his own, he became a frequent visitor at

22
00:01:41.178 --> 00:01:45.502
the Godwin family home during the next two years and struck up a deep friendship with Mary.

23
00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:52.087
They shared a passion for radical thinking and literature and a commitment to the ideals of freedom and

24
00:01:52.127 --> 00:01:59.121
democracy. In 1814, in a surprising turn of events, Shelley left his wife and daughter, eloping to France

25
00:01:59.141 --> 00:01:59.641
with Mary.

26
00:02:00.562 --> 00:02:06.086
Shocked and embarrassed, Mary's father refused to speak to either of them for the next three years, until

27
00:02:06.127 --> 00:02:12.712
they were finally married on December 30, 1816, several months after Percy Shelley's wife committed suicide.

28
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:17.156
Between their elopement and marriage, the young couple moved around Europe.

29
00:02:18.157 --> 00:02:24.013
Mary followed academic pursuits, easily learning French as well as Italian, and reading many of the poets whose

30
00:02:24.073 --> 00:02:25.354
works had inspired Shelley.

31
00:02:26.314 --> 00:02:31.157
When their money began to run low, Shelley, determined to spend his life writing, was forced to go

32
00:02:31.197 --> 00:02:32.537
into hiding from his creditors.

33
00:02:33.718 --> 00:02:39.841
In March 1815, Mary sank into a depression when their first child, a daughter named Clara, died when

34
00:02:39.861 --> 00:02:41.122
she was only a few weeks old.

35
00:02:42.102 --> 00:02:47.485
Percy Shelley, who was involved in his own desires and goals, became distant, spending more time with his

36
00:02:47.545 --> 00:02:49.486
friends and fellow poets than with his wife.

37
00:02:50.947 --> 00:02:56.861
In 1816, however, things began to look up when Mary had a second child, a boy they called

38
00:02:56.921 --> 00:02:58.102
William, after her father.

39
00:02:59.122 --> 00:03:04.943
Shelley also began to have some success with his writing, which Mary often edited and prepared for publication.

40
00:03:05.924 --> 00:03:11.965
Oddly, it was during this happy time following William's birth and the first major publication of Shelley's poetry

41
00:03:12.445 --> 00:03:14.606
that Mary produced her bleak masterpiece.

42
00:03:15.666 --> 00:03:20.387
It was the summer of 1816, and the couple, along with their second child, the infant William, and

43
00:03:20.427 --> 00:03:26.202
Mary's stepsister, Claire Clairmont, went to Geneva to spend time with Lord Byron, an influential poet.

44
00:03:27.002 --> 00:03:30.704
They rented a house on the shores of Lake Geneva and spent the days boating and writing.

45
00:03:31.704 --> 00:03:34.785
Every evening, they would meet at the house and share their writings.

46
00:03:35.726 --> 00:03:37.707
The gatherings were inspiring to each of them.

47
00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:45.910
That summer, Shelley wrote his "Hymn to Intellectual Beauty," a significant achievement, and Byron completed part of his

48
00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.251
"Childe Harold's Pilgrimage."

49
00:03:48.051 --> 00:03:53.357
Clairmont also wrote several poems, but Mary seemed unable to get her thoughts onto paper.

50
00:03:53.837 --> 00:03:57.659
Then, one evening, they were forced to stay inside because of a fierce storm.

51
00:03:58.139 --> 00:04:01.901
Byron suggested that they have a contest to see who could write the best horror story.

52
00:04:02.681 --> 00:04:07.523
Inspired by a dream in which she brought her baby back to life, Mary finally had a breakthrough.

53
00:04:08.224 --> 00:04:14.226
She began writing Frankenstein: or, the Modern Prometheus, a short novel that became her masterpiece.

54
00:04:15.927 --> 00:04:17.288
The story centers on Dr.

55
00:04:17.308 --> 00:04:23.468
Frankenstein, a scientist obsessed with solving the mysteries of nature and giving life to an inanimate body.

56
00:04:24.288 --> 00:04:28.871
He puts a man together out of body parts taken from a graveyard and uses science to bring

57
00:04:28.911 --> 00:04:29.372
him to life.

58
00:04:30.272 --> 00:04:35.536
But Frankenstein is appalled when his creation turns out to be hideous, not the beautiful man he had

59
00:04:35.576 --> 00:04:36.997
imagined. Dr.

60
00:04:37.037 --> 00:04:41.780
Frankenstein quickly abandons his beast, leaving him to fend for himself in the world.

61
00:04:42.841 --> 00:04:48.176
The lonely creature comes across a loving family who treat each other with kindness and respect, and envies

62
00:04:48.217 --> 00:04:48.977
their relationship.

63
00:04:50.078 --> 00:04:56.002
However, when the family rejects him because of his horrid appearance, the monster takes his revenge out on

64
00:04:56.042 --> 00:04:58.643
his creator, killing everyone the doctor loves.

65
00:04:59.704 --> 00:05:00.925
In the end, Dr.

66
00:05:00.965 --> 00:05:07.489
Frankenstein must take responsibility for his creation and chases him into the frigid Arctic, where he spends the

67
00:05:07.529 --> 00:05:09.491
rest of his life trying to catch his monster.

68
00:05:10.712 --> 00:05:15.915
This tragedy is reflected in the title; Prometheus was a Greek god who gave fire to humans and

69
00:05:15.955 --> 00:05:18.488
was forever punished for it—just as Dr.

70
00:05:18.528 --> 00:05:22.690
Frankenstein tried to use technology to create life and suffered for his ambition.

71
00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:28.893
Many literature experts believe that Mary Shelley's story is a warning against the blind pursuit of technology that

72
00:05:28.933 --> 00:05:29.993
characterized her era.

73
00:05:30.013 --> 00:05:35.895
(The early nineteenth century saw the growth of factories, and the invention of railways, gas lighting, and steam

74
00:05:35.955 --> 00:05:38.857
engines, as well as a new disregard for nature.)

75
00:05:39.717 --> 00:05:45.619
On the other hand, many other writers consider Frankenstein to be a reflection of 19-year-old Mary's own loneliness

76
00:05:45.699 --> 00:05:46.820
and desire for a family.

77
00:05:47.656 --> 00:05:53.599
Like the monster she created, Mary Shelley searched for companionship and love but found them elusive.

78
00:05:54.740 --> 00:06:00.123
By the time she was 25, Mary had witnessed the deaths of three of her four children (only

79
00:06:00.263 --> 00:06:04.225
one survived to adulthood) and her young husband, who drowned in a boating accident.

80
00:06:05.246 --> 00:06:11.409
After Percy Shelley's death in 1822, Mary became a champion of his poetry, helping him achieve the great

81
00:06:11.489 --> 00:06:12.990
status that he has to this day.

82
00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:16.552
She also went on to write several more novels of her own.

83
00:06:17.524 --> 00:06:22.426
When Mary Shelley died in 1851, her Frankenstein had already become a classic.

84
00:06:23.387 --> 00:06:27.248
The novel, which some see as a simple horror story and others acclaim as one of the first

85
00:06:27.308 --> 00:06:32.171
works of science fiction, continues to inspire writers and filmmakers around the world.

86
00:06:33.031 --> 00:06:38.974
Whether in the modern literary genre of science fiction or in a Hollywood monster movie, the tale of

87
00:06:39.034 --> 00:06:44.116
Mary Shelley's monster lives on as one of the most recognizable narratives in English literature.
